f:T5fa,{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What should a beginner read first in Indian philosophy?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Most seekers should begin with Vedanta because it introduces the big questions clearly: What is the self? What is reality? What causes suffering? From there, comparisons with Samkhya, Yoga, and Bhakti traditions become much easier to understand."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is ancient Indian philosophy purely intellectual?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. In Sanatan Dharma, philosophy is inseparable from liberation. The purpose of debate is not winning arguments; it is removing confusion so practice becomes precise and realization becomes possible."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the difference between philosophy and tradition in Hinduism?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A philosophy explains reality at the level of concepts and metaphysics. A tradition translates those insights into worship, meditation, ethics, teacher lineages, and lived culture. The two are deeply connected but not identical."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why do Advaita and Dvaita both exist if they read the same scriptures?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Because the shared source texts — especially the Upanishads, Bhagavad Gita, and Brahma Sutras — are rich enough to support different interpretive emphases. Different acharyas draw out different relationships between God, soul, and world."}}]}7:["$","$1","c",{"children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"min-h-screen bg-background text-foreground selection:bg-orange-500/30 selection:text-orange-100 flex flex-col","children":[["$","$Le",null,{"slug":"ancient-wisdom-philosophies","pillar":"pillar-hub"}],["$","script",null,{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"$f"}}],["$","script",null,{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"CollectionPage\",\"name\":\"Ancient Wisdom & Philosophies of Sanatan Dharma\",\"description\":\"A pillar hub for Vedanta, non-duality, classical Darshanas, and beginner guides to Indian philosophy.\",\"url\":\"https://opensadhaka.com/ancient-wisdom-philosophies\",\"hasPart\":[{\"@type\":\"Article\",\"position\":1,\"name\":\"What is Vedanta? A Complete Beginner's Guide\",\"url\":\"https://opensadhaka.com/what-is-vedanta\"},{\"@type\":\"Article\",\"position\":2,\"name\":\"Advaita Vedanta Explained Simply\",\"url\":\"https://opensadhaka.com/advaita-vedanta-explained\"},{\"@type\":\"Article\",\"position\":3,\"name\":\"Non-Duality vs Dualism\",\"url\":\"https://opensadhaka.com/non-duality-vs-dualism\"},{\"@type\":\"Article\",\"position\":4,\"name\":\"Adi Shankaracharya: Life & Legacy\",\"url\":\"https://opensadhaka.com/adi-shankaracharya-life-teachings\"}]}"}}],["$","$L10",null,{}],["$","main",null,{"className":"flex-grow pt-32 pb-16","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"container-padding max-w-5xl mx-auto","children":[["$","header",null,{"className":"mb-20 text-center","children":[["$","h1",null,{"className":"font-display text-5xl md:text-7xl font-bold mb-6 tracking-tight","children":["Ancient ",["$","span",null,{"className":"text-orange-500","children":"Wisdom"}]," & Philosophies"]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-xl text-muted-foreground max-w-3xl mx-auto leading-relaxed","children":"For over 5,000 years, the sages of the Indian subcontinent have rigorously investigated the nature of reality, consciousness, and the self. Explore the profound philosophical frameworks of Sanatan Dharma."}]]}],"$L11","$L12","$L13","$L14","$L15","$L16"]}]}],"$L17"]}],["$L18","$L19"],"$L1a"]}]
